<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    TargetFramework, LangVersion, Nullable, ImplicitUsings, analyzer settings
    and XML doc generation all come from Directory.Build.props.

    This is a runnable project, not a test project, and the distinction is
    ruled rather than stylistic. ../AGENTS.md § Exactness discipline: a target
    with a known answer belongs in a test project and gates CI; a target with
    no known answer belongs here and its output is data. pi^3/zeta(3) has no
    pass or fail, and the run depends on wall-clock time, which a test may not.

    The zeta(2) walk lives here anyway, though its answer IS known. It is not
    a control - Zeta.Tests already carries those - it is the sniff test for
    the presentation, which otherwise has nothing whose output a reader can
    check. Being here costs nothing; being in Zeta.Tests would make a long
    printed table masquerade as a pass.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <RootNamespace>HalHeinrich.Numerics.Experiments</RootNamespace>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>

    <!--
      Same arrangement as Zeta.Tests', for the same reason.
      GenerateDocumentationFile is inherited as true because documentation
      generation is IDE0005's documented prerequisite (roslyn#41640), so
      opting out would make that rule structurally unable to fire here and
      "0 warnings under AnalysisMode=All" would quietly mean less in this
      project than in the library - halheinrich/Math#43. CS1591 is suppressed
      instead: the types here are named rather than documented member by
      member, and the reasoning that matters is in the remarks, not in a
      per-parameter summary.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    One reference, and unlike the library's it does NOT escape this
    repository - Zeta.csproj carries the escaping edge to ..\..\RealConstants
    and everything beyond it arrives transitively through here. So this
    project adds no new checkout requirement; see README.md § Building for the
    four-sibling layout the chain already needs.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Zeta\Zeta.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Presentation makes decisions, and two of them read plausibly when wrong.
    Normalising the blame split is the case that earned this: PowerShare and
    DivisorShare are absolute parts of PropagatedError rather than fractions
    of one, so rendering them directly as percentages prints 0.00% for both
    and looks like a pipeline that has stopped rather than a formatting
    mistake - which is what an umbrella probe of this walk did first time.
    Truncating an exact rational to decimal is the other. Both are pure
    functions of their inputs, so the test project is given sight of them and
    holds them against hand-computed values.

    The same seam now carries the survivor report and the chart geometry,
    which are pure in the same way and fail as quietly: a list of enclosures
    in and a survivor set out, a value and a range in and a pixel out. An
    intersection that filtered on the latest enclosure, or an axis that
    inverted itself, produces output that reads perfectly well and says the
    wrong thing.

    Everything here is internal, which is also what .editorconfig's CA1515
    arrangement expects: that rule is suppressed only under [**/*Tests.cs], so
    a public type in this project would fail the build. The types are an
    implementation of this runner and not a surface anything may build on.

    Testing a formatter does not make this a test project. ../AGENTS.md
    § Exactness discipline separates the two by whether a run has a known
    answer and whether it depends on wall-clock time; a formatter has one and
    does not.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Zeta.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
